From ab40c7ea8a02f855aa8e8e51aa4a5df209be705d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: vincent <vincent@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 05:25:53 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] redact: a Japanese mobile (+81 90-1234-5678) is masked, in a
 CSV and in prose (#3776, yetone)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The rules had one PHONE shape, a Chinese mobile (`(?:\+?86[- ]?)?1[3-9]\d{9}`),
so a Japanese mobile went as written: the reporter's contacts CSV masked both
emails and left `+81 90-1234-5678` in the text. Only the local models could
find it, and AstrLink Guard did not, which is what the eval's GuardKnown for
`tool-result-mixed` was recording (it fails once the value comes out right).

`rules` gains a Japanese mobile beside the Chinese one:
`(?:\+?81[- ]?|0)[789]0[- ]\d{4}[- ]\d{4}`, digits-bounded and personal, so
090/080/070 with the country code or bare is masked and a landline (+81
3-1234-5678) is not. `modelFits`'s PHONE case already takes the shape
(numberLike, 10 digits, a separator, so `strings.Trim(v, digits) != ""`), which
the model sub-case of the new test shows, so nothing there changed. The
GuardKnown for #3776 in eval_cases_test.go is taken off, as its own failure
asked.

Without the rule, `go test -tags nogui ./internal/redact/ -count=1 -run
TestMaskPhoneShapes` fails three cases, the reporter's among them:

    redact_test.go:106: "90-1234-5678" still in "$ cat contacts.csv\nname,email,phone\nRina Kobayashi…

With it that command passes (8 of 8), `TestMaskEval` passes, and the whole
package passes: `go test -tags nogui ./internal/redact/ -count=1` → ok, 95
tests. gofmt and `go vet -tags nogui ./internal/redact/` are clean.
